E1c — is COARSE AIR + FINE WALLS worth it? Measured against exact truth.

E1b died: at size factor 2.5 the loaded geometry self-intersects, because a
single global factor couples two scales that have nothing to do with each other:

    the AIR        ~15 mm elements, carries ~99% of the field, SMOOTH
    the TUBE WALLS  1.0-1.5 mm, carries ~0.06% of TE011's energy, SETS THE FLOOR

🔑 THE TEST BED IS THE TRICK HERE. Build the FULL loaded geometry — all three
tubes, the mode filter — but set every dielectric to eps = 1.0. Vacuum tubes are
electromagnetically INVISIBLE, so THE EXACT CLOSED FORM STILL APPLIES, while the
mesh still has to negotiate every thin wall. That gives a known-truth reference
for a meshing strategy on the real geometry, which no loaded solve can offer.

    air_coarsen  1.0   baseline: air sized from the wavelength
                 2.0   air elements twice as large, walls unchanged
                 3.0
                 4.0

PREDICTION, DECLARED BEFORE THE RUN: the air carries almost all the field, so
coarsening it should HURT — but at solver order 2 the basis is rich enough that
moderate coarsening may cost little. Worthwhile if k = 2-3 holds max|Δ| under
~2 MHz while cutting elements and time by >2x.

🔴 FALSIFIER: if accuracy degrades roughly in proportion to the element saving,
grading buys nothing — it is just a coarser mesh with extra steps, and the
loaded cavity is simply expensive. That is a real possible outcome and it would
close the question rather than leave it open.

VERIFICATION   physics.spectrum(), exact — valid because every dielectric is
               vacuum, so the geometry is EM-transparent.
FALSIFICATION  the exactly-degenerate TE011/TM111 splitting, true value 0.

